  "image_name": "c8fc22dbfe9512b6923f32a2f07c09c8.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual landscape showing how human activity and climate-related pressures combine to drive harmful algal growth in a lake or reservoir. Nutrients, especially nitrogen and phosphorus from livestock, manure, arable agriculture, fertilizer, groundwater, wastewater, and industrial sources, are carried by precipitation, runoff, and treatment-related discharges into connected streams and the main water body. Inside the reservoir, these inputs appear to mix and accumulate.\n\nAt the same time, broader environmental changes are shown increasing stress on the system: greenhouse gas emissions, urbanization, and industry are linked with higher temperature, altered precipitation, and more drought. Wind appears to decrease, while sunlight and dam effects are highlighted as additional controls. Together these factors promote surface warming and stronger water-column stratification, reducing mixing and creating favorable conditions for buoyant algae near the surface.\n\nThe overall message is that eutrophication and climate change act together rather than separately. Nutrient enrichment supplies the material for blooms, while warming, drought, stratification, and reservoir conditions make blooms more likely and persistent. The inset summary on the right reinforces this chain, indicating that increased sunlight, temperature, drought, and dam influence lead to more stratification and therefore greater algae growth.",
